## Important Notes

- **Deletion protection** — the ODB Network, Exadata Infrastructure, Peering,
  VM Cluster and Autonomous VM Cluster modules set `lifecycle { prevent_destroy =
  true }`, so `terraform destroy`, or any change that would force a replacement,
  is refused. To destroy on purpose, remove that line from the module under
  `modules/`, then run `terraform destroy`.
- **Compute/storage counts** on Exadata Infra are ignored after initial creation
  (controlled by Oracle, not Terraform).
- **DB server IDs and SSH keys** on VM Clusters (and DB servers on Autonomous VM
  Clusters) are ignored after creation — the service reshapes them, and they
  would otherwise plan a replacement. Sizing (CPU, memory, storage) is not
  ignored, so a resize in `terraform.auto.tfvars` produces a plan.
- **`db_servers_mode`** picks which DB servers a cluster lands on:
  - `"auto"` (default) — every server on the Exadata Infrastructure. A DB server
    hosts one VM per cluster and reports allocated-vs-total capacity
    (`cpu_core_count`/`max_cpu_count`, `memory_size_in_gbs`/`max_memory_in_gbs`),
    so a server another cluster already sits on is still a valid target while it
    has room. This is the normal way to add a second cluster to an infrastructure.
  - `"unallocated"` — only servers whose `vm_cluster_ids` and
    `autonomous_vm_cluster_ids` are both empty, to give a cluster hardware of its
    own. A `check` block warns at plan time when nothing is left.
  - `"manual"` — exactly the `db_servers` list you supply.
{% if adbs %}
- **Autonomous Database (Serverless)** — arguments the service can choose
  (`db_version`, auto-scaling, backup retention, mTLS, …) are left out of
  `terraform.auto.tfvars` unless set, so the database keeps whatever the service
  assigned and plans stay clean. Setting one makes Terraform manage it.
- **Changing `network_ref`, `character_set` or `ncharacter_set` replaces the
  database.** Review any plan that touches them.
- **`admin_password_source`** — `"write_only"` sends the password through the
  write-only `admin_password_wo`; rotate it by changing the password *and*
  bumping `admin_password_wo_version`. `"secrets_manager"` has OCI read it from
  AWS Secrets Manager through `secret_iam_role_arn`, which needs the account's
  Secrets Manager integration enabled.
{% endif %}
{% if adb_integration %}
- **The Secrets Manager integration is account- and Region-wide.** Creating it
  adopts one that is already enabled; destroying it would disable Secrets
  Manager passwords for every Autonomous Database in the account and Region,
  including ones outside this configuration. The module sets `prevent_destroy`.
  To stop managing it here, drop it from state instead:
  ```hcl
  removed {
    from = module.aws_adb_secrets_integration
    lifecycle { destroy = false }
  }
  ```
{% endif %}
